s fire and rehire these contracts  that have been imposed make us workers  worse off the main thing with the  contracts in tower hamlets  is a reduction in severance by 80  percent  um cuts to travel allowance for myself  i'm a low-paid worker  so for me that's a reduction of 596  pounds to my salary  makes you feel disrespected i think  that's the biggest part  we were you know so many of us in the  council like myself we've been we've  been key workers we're essential workers  we've been working from home going out  doing visits you know where we  absolutely have to  wearing the necessary ppe  but then at the same time you know  you've got people clapping on thursdays  and you've got the council clapping  but then you're being fired and rehired  and being put on worse contracts that  makes your  terms and conditions much worse the  dispute  doesn't seem for you to be just about  money it's about who you are of course  you know for myself um i'm a lower paid  worker in the council  and it's just is this the gratitude that  you get for putting your life on the  line during the pandemic to support the  community  tell hamlets councils say the new  contracts were only introduced after a  long period of consultation  the severance package they say remains  one of the most generous for council  workers in london  it prevents big payouts to high earners  and any money saved has been reinvested  into pay for staff  but this issue goes far beyond one  employer  research by the tuc earlier this year  found that nine percent of workers have  been told to reapply for their jobs  on worst terms and conditions since the  first lockdown in march last year  that figure doubles to 18 for young  people aged 18 to 24.  and workers from minority ethnic  backgrounds have been almost  twice as likely as white workers to have  faced fire and rehire  with 15 percent saying they've been  affected  and there's been widespread criticism of  the tactic including from the most  senior conservatives we believe that  using threats of firing and re-hiring is  unacceptable  as a negotiating tactic and there are  laws in place  uh to ensure that contractual uh  conditions uh  can't discriminate uh against people on  grounds of race or sexual  or disability now a former labour front  ventures trying to change the law  he's proposing a bill to clamp down on  fire and rehire  it's to stop managers intimidating the  workforce by saying  you're fired but if you want your back  job back in the morning  you have to sign these new terms and  conditions which are going to cut your  pay  cut your pension rights and and  going for the nuclear option right from  the beginning  what i want to see is proper  consultation  proper negotiation i want britain to be  the best place  for people to work not not to have this  bully boy tactics  i'm talking about some workers who were  told that this was going to be 12  000 pounds off their annual salary i'm  talking about  you know teachers who were told that it  was ripping up their pension rights  so we do need to see that this is  something that shouldn't be happening  and i'm i'm pleased actually that you  know if you speak  or listen to jacob reese morgan and  boris johnson  they both said that this is unacceptable  trouble is they've so far not been  willing to legislate  why do we need law on this surely  this is one where as you were saying we  get people  round the table try to engender some  trust  talk about it the issue is this no  employer believes that it is morally  right  simply to say to your workforce you're  fired  you accept 15 20 000 pounds  off your annual wage or you're out and  they don't there's no question about  that they know it's morally wrong  but because the law doesn't give them  any duty  to negotiate and to consult they don't  most of the employers who are in  disputes in this  situation tend to say the following  we're we've been faced with covid we're  faced with technological change and  disruption  we've got terms and conditions that have  lasted all 5  10 15 years in the end  we're left with a choice do we declare  redundancies  do we just collapse the company or do we  try  to get new terms many of the companies  who are doing this have actually made  record profits during the kogit pandemic  in response the government says we are  absolutely clear that using  fire and rehire as a negotiating tactic  is completely  unacceptable and we expect companies to  treat their employees  fairly if an employer needs to make  changes to an employee's contract  they must reach a formal agreement with  that worker or their representatives  we've asked acas to produce clearer and  more comprehensive guidance for business  a government commissioned review already  carried out by acas  found that there may be an increase in  fire and debris higher  when covered economic support measures  wind down  a process that began this month it's a  reminder that workers losing their jobs  hasn't been the only result of the  pandemic even for some of those still in  work  things are feeling very different  in response to our inquiry tower  hamlet's council sent us this statement  we have consulted extensively with our  staff since january 2019  and introduced revised terms and  conditions in july 2020  among the changes was a reduction to our  severance scheme  which remains one of the most generous  in london local  government the new terms and conditions  mean money has been diverted back into  improving pay for staff  working for the council no staff are on  worst conditions  or pay or have been dismissed as a  result of the changes  and we think they offer a fair deal for  our valued  staff now today is  nelson mandela day and it comes at a  time when many south africans may be  looking at the state of their nation  and wondering what the world's favorite  revolutionary would make of it  the jailing of former south african  president jacob  zuma earlier this month sparked  wi