n your father came out of poles more  prison  and i uh commentated on it with taboo  and becky who became his deputy  and the first president and i don't  think on that day  anybody imagine though we knew things  would be difficult  that south africa would be where it is  today question i really want to ask you  is  is this for the last 27 years the  movement that your father  and oliver tambo created the african  national  congress has been in government how much  of what is happening today  is the anc's responsibility  i would say all of which is actually the  responsibility of the aac  the anc seems to have forgotten why it  came into power  it came into power mainly  because it wanted to improve the  conditions and lives of black people  who have been impoverished they are  dignity taken away by the apartheid  regime  political freedom was never enough  my father knew it they knew it and  we cannot leave the economy that  supported apartheid  to be intact because there is no way  therefore  to redress the iniquities of the past  and address the conditions of the poor i  i mean if you look  at the conditions of the poor in south  africa  who are mainly black and it has not  improved  alexander when my death stage when he  came to join respect he's still the same  which has not improved  simple things like providing adequate  housing  water and clean services  has been something that this government  has failed actually our people in terms  to deliver  and so um what is happening right now  is squarely in their hands as my father  said  before with apartheid they cannot go on  forever  looking at peaceful means and the  government doesn't hear  those who are poor who have not  benefited from what i call the  post-apartheid dividend  have been poor for more than 300 years  their father their generations their  grandfather their great grandfather  how long should they wait until  conditions improve for them  nobody enter i want to assure you  wants to see south africa in the  situation of violence  but unless south africa squarely address  the issues of poverty and inequity  youth unemployment which is hovering at  about 74  south africa will see more of this  violence occurring  more and more dr mandela  thank you very much in indeed um i hope  today  uh is a good day for you at least thank  you  thank you very much trevor  as we've been discussing lockdown  restrictions in england will be lifted  tomorrow  good news from any business you may  think but a poll of business leaders  carried out by the chartered management  institute and shared  exclusively with this show has found  that half believe  the government is lifting restrictions  too quickly  39 believe it's happening at the right  pace  43 percent of managers said some staff  who  are currently working remotely will be  asked to return to the office  a quarter said they'd ask most staff  while only 13 will be asking all staff  to return  over three three-quarters of managers  will continue with covid19 safety  measures in the workplace  despite the lifting of restrictions  tomorrow only two percent will  not and there were concerns about the  impact of self-isolation  something we've been discussing this  morning 53 percent of managers were  concerned that the amount of staff  self-isolating will impact business  operations  46 weren't concerned well  to talk more about this we're joined by  the chief executive of the cmi  anne frank and frank um  half of respondents uh think that it's  all going too fast  why is there an explanation well i think  the explanation  is um quite apparent um as we know  cases are uh surging they're uh  they're um 50 000 and of course britain  now has  um the highest the third highest number  of cases in the world  um and equally we also know  that um many many many people are being  contacted i think it's up 46  week on week and asking to self-isolate  indeed um  the secretary of state for health for  example uh has contacted covet and t